    "id": "zander_tx_buddy",
    "title": {
      "en": "#6 Prof. Zander et al. TX Antenna Comparison: Short Portable Vertical vs 5/8 λ Vertical"
    },
    "description": {
      "en": "[Publication: Zander, J. (2022). Simple HF antenna efficiency comparisons using the WSPR system](https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.08989)\n\n**How does a short portable transmitting antenna compare with a larger vertical?** This demo reproduces **Experiment A, Figure 4**, comparing **SK0WE/P’s 2.5 m center-loaded Difona vertical as Target** with **SK0WE/1’s Hy-Gain AV620 as Reference**. Both stations are at **JO97**. The simultaneous **Hardware A/B** comparison uses their **20 m** transmissions on **21 May 2022, 09:30–10:30 UTC**.\n\nWSPRadar matches reports of both transmitters decoded by the **same remote receiving station and reported locator in the same two-minute WSPR cycle**. Each matched observation is a **Joint Spot**, representing one receiver and one cycle. The compared quantity is the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), and **ΔSNR = Target − Reference**: positive values favour the short Difona vertical, negative values favour the Hy-Gain AV620, and 0 dB means equal SNR. Reports of only one transmitter cannot provide this paired difference.\n\nIn **TX Benchmark**, open **Segment Inspector → Benchmark Evidence** and keep all distance ranges and directions selected. Compare the publication with the **right-hand Joint-Spot Δ SNR histogram**, where each Joint Spot contributes one value. The **middle Station Medians (Δ SNR) histogram** answers a different question: each receiver contributes one median, giving receivers equal weight instead of giving more weight to receivers with more matched observations.\n\nThe **166 joint receiver-cycles** give a **mean ΔSNR of −6.78 dB** and a **sample standard deviation of 3.51 dB**, essentially identical to the paper’s **−6.8 dB** and **3.5 dB**. The standard deviation describes the spread of the individual differences around their mean. The negative mean indicates that the sampled paths strongly favour the Reference vertical.\n\n[**Figure 4 comparison (PDF)**](app/static/reference_figures/zander_fig4_paper_v1/WSPRadar_Demo_Zander_Figure4.pdf): Compare the publication in **Panel A**, the reconstruction using the paper’s bins in **Panel B**, and WSPRadar’s histogram in **Panel C**. WSPRadar uses finer **1 dB bins** with bars showing the **percentage of matched observations**. The paper uses coarser bins and **probability density**, which also accounts for bin width. The bars therefore look somewhat different; regrouping the WSPRadar observations into the paper’s bins and converting to density reconciles the two presentations.\n\n**What else can you discover in Map View?** Look beyond the overall average: compare the **west-northwestern paths to North America** with the **western and southwestern paths across Europe**. The sector colours show how relative SNR varies with direction and distance. Each coloured sector shows the median of its receivers’ median ΔSNR values, giving every qualifying receiver equal weight. In this reconstruction, the Reference advantage is larger toward south-southwestern Europe than toward west-southwestern Europe or North America. Use **Segment Inspector** to select these directions and compare the receiver counts and Joint Spots, then explore individual paths in **Station Insights**. This is a directional pattern in the sampled relative performance, not a complete antenna radiation pattern. Uncoloured sectors provide no supported ΔSNR estimate; they do not demonstrate antenna nulls.\n\nUse the matched reports to assess **relative performance on the sampled radio paths**. This result does not establish direction-independent antenna gain."
    }
  },
